  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Daily precipitation chances today and tomorrow with \na strong cold front yielding a chance at severe weather on\nTuesday.\n\nAs heat and humidity increases today and tomorrow, instability \nincreases with daily shower and thunderstorm chances. Deep \nupper level troughing remains over the eastern CONUS through \nTuesday. Increasing instability and shear will yield some \nconvection capable of being stronger and more organized. \nTherefore, SPC has portions of the forecast area in a marginal \nrisk for severe weather today with the entire forecast area in a\nmarginal risk for severe weather on Tuesday. Primary hazard \ntype each day will be damaging wind gusts. \n\nOn Tuesday, while the deep upper level trough pivots east across\nthe forecast area, a strong area of surface low pressure tracks\nfrom the Great Lakes region towards New England. The associated\nwarm front lifts through the forecast area early Tuesday \nmorning ahead of the potent cold front pushing through later \nTuesday afternoon and into the evening. Despite morning rain \nshowers with the warm front, guidance continues to show plenty\nof instability. Combined with ample shear, strong to severe\nthunderstorms are expected. Currently primary hazard type looks\nto be damaging wind gusts with heavy rainfall capable of Flash\nFlooding. SPC has the entire forecast area with the exception\nfor western MD, in a slight risk for severe weather. The rest of\nthe forecast area is in a marginal risk. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Drier conditions return mid to late week.\n\nIn the wake of Tuesday's frontal boundary, the associated low will \nslowly meander towards the northeast over the next couple of days. \nThis should reduce rain chances overall, but spotty showers & \nthunderstorms remain possible as this low slowly slides offshore of \nNew England late in the week. High pressure moves in overhead \ntowards the end of the week, bringing drier air and similar \nconditions overall as the week prior, with highs in the low-to-mid \n80s. \n\nHeading into next weekend, a broad area of low pressure begins to \nform over the Midwest and track eastward. As temperatures gradually \nrise towards more normal July averages, this could bring additional \nshower & thunderstorm chances to the region.",
